      "group_4 (our submission) is a pure-IDM run: 0 emergency episodes, minimum spacing 143.3 units, dimensionless v0~=0.564, a~=0.075, T~=5.307.",
      "groups that needed the safety layer (>=1 emergency episode): ['group_1', 'group_2']; groups that stayed in pure IDM control: ['group_3', 'group_4', 'group_5'].",
      "group_1 fires its emergency flag at large spacing (min spacing over the run 434.6 units); its triggers are therefore NOT proximity-driven, so ranking it least-safe by raw episode count is misleading. proximity-driven=False.",
      "group_2 is the only run whose spacing reaches the floor (min spacing = 0.0) and whose emergency triggers coincide with spacing 0-5.5 units: it is the genuinely proximity-critical run.",
      "the mean commanded speed (v_cmd) is a scale-dependent quantity and is confounded across groups; the speed-based efficiency ordering is descriptive only, unlike the moving fraction, which is dimensionless and comparable.",
      "no monotone relation holds between the dimensionless combinations and the safety-layer usage: group_1 (smallest v0~ and a~) and group_2 (largest T~) sit at different corners of the box yet both triggered the layer."
    ],
    "caveats": [
      "each group ran a different physical car / IP (metadata.json leader_ip / follower_ip), so run-to-run differences confound parameter differences",
      "one run per group: no repetition, therefore no inferential claim, only descriptive ordering",
      "the raw parameter scales are inconsistent between groups (different units/gauges), so only the dimensionless combinations are comparable",
      "device / recording-scale differences (coordinate-unit speed vs vehicle-reported speed) are not removable from these logs"
    ]
